{"answer":"The ledger is kept by all of us together. Each fisher writes their haul before the outing and a chosen keeper—usually the most experienced—records it in the book. The keeper is not a single person but a rotating duty, so no one can cheat. After the outing, the keeper checks the catch physically: he holds the net, counts the fish and weighs them in the field. The numbers written match the actual catch, and everyone is present to confirm. If a number is wrong, the fisher is asked to explain and the record is corrected. This way we all know the ledger is true because it is written and verified by everyone together.

We do not keep any digital records, just a small notebook on the boat and a chalkboard at the dock. Everyone can see it and cross‑check.

